						    <h1 class="page-title ">
        Measure N    </h1>

							
							
<div class="entry-content">
	<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>MEASURE N</strong><br />
<strong>CITY OF CLAYTON</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">CITYWIDE LANDSCAPE MAINTENANCE EXTENSION AND ALTERATION OF RATE OF EXISTING SPECIAL TAX<br />
Shall the measure to fund on-going trail system operations and maintenance, roadway landscaping, open space weed abatement, and related services of the Clayton Community Facility District No. 2007-1 (Citywide Landscape Maintenance) by extending the special tax for ten years and amending its rate and methodology to $354.45/year per residential parcel and $354.45/year per non-residential acre, including annual inflationary adjustments, subject to citizen’s committee oversight, be approved?</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>CITY ATTORNEY’S IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE N</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The City of Clayton has established Community Facilities District 2007-1 within the City (“CFD”), which is authorized to levy a special parcel tax on real property within the CFD to fund City trail system operations and maintenance, roadway landscaping, open space weed abatement, and related services. Pursuant to City Measure “H”, the CFD currently levies the tax at the annual rate of $312.84 per residential parcel and $312.84 per acre, or fraction thereof, for non-residential parcels. The tax started collection on July 1, 2007 and will expire on June 30, 2027, unless extended by voters.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">After conducting a noticed public hearing on July 7, 2026, the City Council unanimously voted to place Measure N on the November 3, 2026 General Municipal Election ballot. It proposes to extend the CFD parcel tax for ten (10) years, with a new expiration date of June 30, 2037. It also proposes to increase the annual parcel tax rate, starting July 1, 2027, to $354.45 per residential parcel and $354.45 per acre, or fraction thereof, for non-residential parcels.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The Measure allows the City Council to increase the tax rate, starting July 1, 2028 and in each fiscal year thereafter by the applicable Consumer Price Index, with a 3% annual cap, after making such a determination at a regular public meeting and after receiving a prior recommendation from the City’s Citizens’ Trails and Landscaping Committee.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The tax will expire on June 30, 2037, unless further extended by Clayton voters at a future election. Measure N does not change the purposes of the parcel tax. Tax revenues will continue to be deposited into a special City fund and used solely to fund City trail system operations and maintenance, roadway landscaping, open space weed abatement, and related services.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Measure N incorporates citizen’s committee oversight. This CFD parcel tax would be collected in the same time and manner and subject to the same requirements as other property taxes fixed and collected by the County of Contra Costa on behalf of the City of Clayton.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Measure N requires the approval of two-thirds (2/3) of Clayton voters voting on the Measure to pass.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">A “yes” vote on Measure N will approve this CFD parcel tax.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">A “no” vote on Measure N will not approve this CFD parcel tax.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><strong>The above statement is an impartial analysis of Measure N. If you desire a copy of the Measure, please call the Office of the City Clerk at (925) 673-7300 and a copy will be mailed at no cost to you.</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">/s/ Malathy Subramanian<br />
City Attorney</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE N</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In 1997, Clayton established the Trails and Landscape Maintenance District (District) to fund the maintenance of trails, open space, weed abatement, firebreaks, and roadside landscaping. A 10-year parcel tax was adopted, providing dedicated funding for Clayton’s landscaping.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In 2007, a citizens’ Blue Ribbon Committee reviewed the District’s successes and challenges. The importance of Clayton’s “Front Yard” was reaffirmed, and a new plan was recommended. It included a Trails and Landscape Committee (TLC), a citizen advisory body appointed by the City Council, to advise the Council and staff on maintenance, improvements, and financial matters related to the Landscape Maintenance District (LMD). Voters overwhelmingly extended the parcel tax in 2007 and 2017, ensuring continued investment in Clayton’s medians, parks, trails, and open spaces.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In FY 2026-2027, residents currently pay $312.84/year per parcel and nonresidents $312.84/per acre or fraction thereof. The LMD maintains approximately 2 million square feet of roadway landscaping, 40,700 square feet of lawns and flowerbeds, 515 acres of open space, 27 miles of trails, 50 street islands and medians, and roughly 3,000 City trees. The LMD funds also support critical fire mitigation efforts in open spaces and around trails, including weed abatement. Evolving fire safety requirements are expected to increase these costs, making dedicated funding increasingly important.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Measure N includes a one-time, 10% reserve fee dedicated to capital improvements and longstanding deferred maintenance. For FY 2027-2028, residents will pay no more than $354.45/year per parcel and nonresidents $354.45/year per acre or fraction thereof, generating $1.5 million annually through 2037, with continued oversight by the TLC.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Clayton’s modest General Fund cannot adequately fund public landscaping and trails. Without this dedicated funding, maintenance would rapidly decline, forever changing Clayton’s beauty and character.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Please support Measure N and essential fire mitigation efforts, and preserve Clayton’s landscaping, trails, open spaces, and community character for future generations.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Holly Tillman, Councilmember<br />
Kim Trupiano, Councilmember<br />
Richard G. Enea, Vice-Mayor<br />
Jeff Wan, Mayor<br />
Jim Diaz, Councilmember</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE N</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Proponents call <strong>Measure N</strong> a “renewal.” It is also a tax <span style="text-decoration: underline;">increase</span>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Clayton’s existing landscape tax is $312.84 per residential parcel. <strong>Measure N</strong> raises it to $354.45 in 2027-28—an immediate 13.3% increase. Then the City Council may increase the tax with inflation by up to 3% every year through 2037. Maximum increases will likely be imposed, and the annual charge would reach about $462 by the final year, and a homeowner would pay more than $4,000 over the ten-year term.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The proponents describe a “one-time 10% reserve fee.” But this is not a one-time payment. It is built into the higher tax base and can itself be increased in later years.</p>
<p>Nor does Clayton’s own budget show an immediate landscape-maintenance crisis. The FY2027 adjusted budget projects about $1.362 million in LMD revenue against $1.386 million in expenditures—a difference of only $24,000—and an ending fund balance of roughly $252,000.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Clayton should justify the size of this increase, control costs, and offer voters a renewal closer to actual needs—not a 13% increase followed by authority for repeated increases.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">This money would be better spent by property owners obtaining residential and commercial fire insurance at home. Inflation will continue, making it necessary for the City to join its residents in belt-tightening. Sooner is better to cut something less necessary.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><strong>Vote No on Measure N.</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Libertarian Party of Contra Costa County, Sandra Kallander, Chair</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE N</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">We know from experience or hearsay (depending on age) that last midcentury, two dimes would buy a gallon of gas or two scoops of ice cream; ten dimes could buy 6 basic hamburgers. If the dimes are silver, they still do. But the digital and paper currencies aren’t doing as well.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">You may be aware that Japan was bailed out by the U.S. because they are our biggest buyer of Treasurys and need money, but if they sell our debt, it devalues faster.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">What does this have to do with this proposed Special Tax? It would rise with inflation. The United States of America is inflating like crazy, and they’re just getting started.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The next ten years are likely to be challenging for everyone.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Perhaps the landscaping budget could be funded by sponsorships, voluntary neighborhood workdays, fundraisers.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Also bad: spending public funds to defend against violations of election code.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">California Elections Code section 13119(a) requires ballots to use this language:</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span style="text-decoration: underline;">“Shall the measure (stating the nature thereof) be adopted?”</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">“The nature thereof” does not mean “why,” or “how,” or “for whom,” or anything other than “what is the nature” of the measure being adopted.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Contra Costa County wrote a ballot question recently. It was ruled “prejudicial and argumentative” in Superior Court. The court ordered offending language removed, including “independent citizens oversight.”</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Section 9051(e) requires it “shall neither be an argument, nor be likely to create prejudice, for or against the proposed measure.”</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Clayton’s <strong>Measure N</strong> ballot question lists benefits, not costs like pay-raises; it is not neutral.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">If you don’t like public funds spent defending violations, the best way to save court costs and stop cheating, is <strong>vote “No”</strong> every time you see a ballot question containing sales pitches. Save that for the argument.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Libertarian Party of Contra Costa County, Sandra Kallander, Chair</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE N</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Opponents of Measure N mischaracterize its purpose and impact. Measure N is not about national inflation, foreign debt, or politics. It is a local solution to maintain Clayton’s landscaping, parks, trails, and open spaces, helping keep our community safe, clean, and attractive. The question before voters is how to responsibly fund these services in the years ahead.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The cost of maintaining more than 500 acres of open space, 27 miles of trails, landscaping, tree trimming, irrigation systems, weed abatement, fire mitigation, and other public areas has increased significantly over time. When funding remains fixed while labor, water, fuel, materials, and contract costs rise, service levels can decline. Measure N is designed to provide a sustainable local funding source that helps maintain existing service levels as costs change.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Suggestions that volunteers or sponsorships can replace dedicated funding are unrealistic. While community involvement is valuable and appreciated, it cannot provide the consistent resources required for year-round maintenance. These services depend on trained personnel, specialized equipment, insurance, accountability, and reliable funding.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Concerns about ballot language should be evaluated based on the actual text of Measure N and applicable election law. California law provides procedures for resolving disputes regarding ballot language and compliance.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Ultimately, Measure N is about stewardship and community responsibility. It will provide a stable, transparent funding source dedicated to preserving Clayton’s parks, trails, landscaped areas, and open space. Voting <strong>Yes on Measure N</strong> helps ensure that Clayton continues to reflect the pride residents take in their community and remains a place where families and businesses can thrive.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Kim Trupiano, Councilmember<br />
Holly Tillman, Councilmember<br />
Richard G. Enea, Vice-Mayor<br />
Jeff Wan, Mayor<br />
Jim Diaz, Councilmember</p>
